\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Manipulation of ellipsoid body neurons R2 and R4 extends lifespan in female and male flies.} \textbf  {(A)} Flies were mated for two days, then separated by sex and sorted 20/vial onto all-trans retinal food for 24 hours before optogenetic treatment began. \textbf  {(B--D)} (Left) Expression patterns of drivers used in each experiment. Neurons were either activated (dashed red lines) using UAS-CsChrimson, or inhibited (dashed green lines) using UAS-GtACR1. Dark controls (black lines) were included for all experiments. P-values were obtained by log-rank analysis of correspondingly colored lines. Control genotypes (solid lines) were bred by crossing respective GAL4 drivers with WCS. \textbf  {(B)} Activation of R2 extended lifespan in both sexes. n = 95-117. \textbf  {(C)} Inhibition of R4d extended lifespan in both sexes. n = 145-189. \textbf  {(D)} Simultaneous activation of R2 and R4 neurons extends lifespan in both sexes. n = 127-139. \textbf  {(E)} Inhibition (left) and activation (right) of broader EB extends lifespan in females. GtACR1 n = 127-133; CsChr n = 95-115. }}{17}{}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:lifespanfig}{{1}{17}{}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {EB manipulations produce distinct head transcriptomes.} Bulk RNA-seq data from whole heads of female flies treated with light for 1 week. Ten heads collected per sample. DEGs identified with a cutoff of p < 0.01 using DESeq2 R package. \textbf  {(A)} DEGs downregulated (left) and upregulated (right) by R2 activation (R2>Chr). \textbf  {(B)} Top 15 GO Biological Processes upregulated by R2 activation. \textbf  {(C)} Top 11 GO Biological Processes downregulated by R2 activation. \textbf  {(D)} DEGs driven by R4d inhibition (R4d>GtACR1). }}{18}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seqfig}{{2}{18}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {R2/R4 activation shares little transcriptomic overlap with R2 activation or R4d inhibition.} Bulk RNA-seq data from whole heads of female flies treated with light for 1 week. Ten heads collected per sample. DEGs identified with a cutoff of p < 0.01 using DESeq2 R package. \textbf  {(A)} DEGs downregulated (left) and upregulated (right) by R2/R4 activation (R2/R4>Chr). \textbf  {(B)} Top 15 GO Biological Processes upregulated by R2/R4 activation. \textbf  {(C)} Top 7 GO Biological Processes downregulated by R2/R4 activation. \textbf  {(D)} Overlap between upregulated head genes in all 3 manipulations. \textbf  {(E)} Overlap between downregulated head genes in all 3 manipulations. }}{19}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seqfig2}{{3}{19}{}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {R2 and R2/R4 activation uncouples nutrient-state signaling from behavioral and physiological outputs.} \textbf  {(A-D)} Interaction (Light $\times $ Genotype) p-values derived from 2-way ANOVAs. Benjamini-Hochberg correction was applied within each sex and driver, and across time points for con-ex. Female TAG points represent 5 flies; all others represent 10 flies. \textbf  {(A)} R2-GAL4 con-ex. R2-activated females were indistinguishable from controls. Naive and one-week pre-treated males were indistinguishable from controls, but male feeding was reduced after one month of pre-treatment Female n = 8-10; male n = 7-10. \textbf  {(B)} R2/R4-GAL4 con-ex. R2/R4 activated males and females were indistinguishable from controls at all time points. Female n = 7-10; male n = 18-20 (naïve), 6-10 (1 week), 4-6 (1 month). \textbf  {(C)} R2-GAL4 TAG levels were reduced for males, but not females. n = 10 for both sexes. \textbf  {(D)} R2/R4-GAL4 TAG levels were reduced for both sexes. Female n = 9-10; male n = 8-10. \textbf  {(E-F)} Starvation assays for R2-GAL4 and R2/R4-GAL4 females and males. P-values were obtained by log-rank analysis of correspondingly colored lines. n = 96-100. }}{20}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:conexfig}{{4}{20}{}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {R2 and R2/R4 activation does not significantly affect motor activity.} Drosophila Activity Monitor System (DAMS) analysis of activity over a 48-hour period. 12:12 light:dark (treatment from ZT 0-12). Interaction (Light $\times $ Genotype) p-values were derived from 2-way ANOVAs. Benjamini-Hochberg correction was applied within each sex and driver and across time points. \textbf  {(A)} Average beam crossings of naïve (left), one week pre-treated (center), and one month pre-treated R2-GAL4 flies. n = 13-16. \textbf  {(B)} Average beam crossings of naïve (left), one week pre-treated (center), and one month pre-treated R2/R4-GAL4 flies. n = 32. }}{21}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:damsfig}{{5}{21}{}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {EB manipulation does not impair climbing ability.} Height reached by 9 sec after drop (top plots of each panel) and total up distance climbed in 30 sec after drop (bottom plots). Interaction (Light $\times $ Genotype) p-values were derived from 2-way ANOVAs. Benjamini-Hochberg correction was applied within each sex and driver and across time points and climbing metrics. Points represent average of 3 trials per individual. n = 23-28. \textbf  {(A-B)} One week of activation has no effect on R2 and R2/R4 males and females. Female R2/R4 flies exhibit improved climbing by both metrics after one month of activation; R2 females, R2 males, and R2/R4 males exhibit no change after one month. \textbf  {(C)} R4d>GtACR1 males, but not females, show improved climbing ability after both one week and five weeks of inhibition. }}{22}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ddropfig}{{6}{22}{}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {FLIC captures distinct valence states induced by EB manipulation.} Sucrose interaction p-values were derived from the Genotype $\times $ Linkage interaction term of a linear mixed model (Interactions ~ Genotype * Linkage + (1|Group), lmer) with yoked pairs modeled as a random intercept. Benjamini-Hochberg correction was applied within each sex and driver and across time points. \textbf  {(A)} A "trigger" fly (top chamber) controlled optogenetic light for itself and a yoked control (bottom) by touching a 10\% sucrose (S10) well. \textbf  {(B)} R2 activation was aversive in light-naïve flies and in flies pre-treated with light for 1 month. n = 9-18. \textbf  {(C)} R4d activation was transiently aversive in flies pre-treated with light for 1 week. n = 13-18. \textbf  {(D)} After correction, R2/R4 flies were indistinguishable from yoked controls. Before correction, light-naïve flies found the well rewarding (p = 0.020). n = 10-18. }}{23}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flicfig}{{7}{23}{}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Valence of EB manipulation is sex-, circuit-, and context-dependent.} \textbf  {(A)} Light-naïve flies (n = 34-36) were placed in lanes with a dark side and an illuminated side (red light, 48 Hz) and subjected to a two-phase protocol: 1) a 10-min dark acclimation period; 2) a 60-min treatment of 5 sec on/5 sec off. Reported p-values for Preference Index correspond to Genotype $\times $ Phase interaction term. \textbf  {(B)} R2/R4>Chr females and males strongly preferred lit side of arena. \textbf  {(C)} R2>Chr females, but not males, preferred dark side. \textbf  {(D)} R4d>Chr females preferred lit side, but showed decreased light preference relative to controls. Males preferred dark side. }}{24}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:arenafig}{{8}{24}{}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {R2 activation produces transcriptional signatures in the periphery that are distinct from those in the head.} Bulk RNA-seq data from whole bodies of R2-GAL4 flies treated with light for 1 week. Five bodies collected per sample. DEGs identified with a cutoff of p < 0.01 using DESeq2 R package. \textbf  {(A)} Overlap between all DEGs observed in R2-activated heads (purple) and bodies (green). Shared genes are indicated by 2 red arrows (upregulated) or a red and blue arrow (oppositely regulated in heads and bodies; red = upregulated in heads, blue = downregulated in heads). \textbf  {(B)} Top 15 peripheral GO Biological Processes upregulated by R2 activation. \textbf  {(C)} Top 15 peripheral GO Biological Processes downregulated by R2 activation. }}{25}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bodyseqfig}{{9}{25}{}{figure.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces \mbox  {{\def \textcolor {yellow}{\textcolor {cyan!30}}\texthl  {(finish this) }}} \textbf  {R2 activation remodels peripheral metabolism.} Targeted metabolomic profiling with a cutoff of p < 0.05. 30 bodies collected per sample. \textbf  {(A)} PC1 separates R2-activated flies from all other samples. \textbf  {(B)} }}{26}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:metabfig}{{10}{26}{}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {R4d activation modestly extends lifespan in males and females.} Flies were mated for two days, then separated by sex and sorted onto all-trans retinal food for 24 hours before optogenetic treatment began. Neurons were activated using UAS-CsChrimson (red lines). Dark controls (black lines) were included for all experiments. P-values were obtained by log-rank analysis of line pairs indicated by color. n = 180-193.}}{28}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lifespansupp}{{S1}{28}{}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {DDrop supplement.} Height reached by 9 sec after drop (top plots of each panel) and total up distance climbed in 30 sec after drop (bottom plots). Interaction (Light $\times $ Genotype) p-values were derived from 2-way ANOVAs. Benjamini-Hochberg correction was applied within each sex and driver and across time points and climbing metrics. Points represent average of 3 trials per individual. n = 20-24. \textbf  {(A)} R4d activation has no effect on climbing ability of females or males. \textbf  {(B)} Broader EB inhibition has no effect on climbing ability of females or males. }}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ddropsupp}{{S2}{29}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Self-administration of optogenetic activation in FLIC drives nuanced appetitive and aversive behaviors.} \textbf  {(A, C-E)} P-values were derived from the Genotype $\times $ Linkage interaction term of a linear mixed model (Outcome ~ Genotype * Linkage + (1|Group), lmer) with yoked pairs modeled as a random intercept. \textbf  {(A)} Naive R2>Chr flies spent less time at S10 well per visit (left), but approached it as often as controls (right). n = 13-18. \textbf  {(B)} (Left) A "trigger" fly (top chamber, R2>WCS) controlled optogentic light for itself, a yoked genetic control (middle chamber), and a yoked R2>Chr fly (bottom chamber) by touching a 10\% sucrose (S10) well. (Right) Naive R2>Chr flies did not interact with well more often than yoked genetic controls, indicating no global, non-associative effects of R2 activation on feeding. P-values were derived from planned contrasts on a linear mixed model (Interactions ~ Condition + (1|Group), lmer) with the yoked pairing structure modeled as a random intercept. n = 16-18. \textbf  {(C)} Naive R4d>GtACR1 interactions were indistinguishable from controls, as all flies were startled by onset of green light. n = 13-18. \textbf  {(D)} Naive R4d>Chr trigger flies, like R2 flies, spent less time at the well per visit (left) but returned as frequently as controls (right). n = 13-17. \textbf  {(E)} R2/R4>Chr trigger flies spent the same amount of time per visit at wells (left) but visited more frequently than controls (right). n = 10-18. }}{30}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flicsupp}{{S3}{30}{}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Stimulation frequency modulates EB-driven valence.} Light-naive flies were placed in lanes with a dark side and an illuminated side (red light at 200 Hz or green light at 40 Hz) and subjected to a two-phase protocol: 1) a 10-min dark acclimation period; 2) a 60-min treatment of 5 sec on/5 sec off. Reported p-values for Preference Index correspond to Genotype $\times $ Phase interaction term. \textbf  {(A)} R2/R4>Chr females and males preferred lit side. n = 34-36. \textbf  {(B)} R2>Chr females preferred unlit side. Males were indistinguishable from controls. n = 35-36. \textbf  {(C)} R4d>GtACR1 females (n = 26-27) and males (n = 17-18) were indistinguishable from controls. \textbf  {(D)} R4d>Chr females and males were indistinguishable from controls. n = 34-36. }}{31}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:arenasupp}{{S4}{31}{}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces \mbox  {{\def \textcolor {yellow}{\textcolor {cyan!30}}\texthl  {(finish this)}}} \textbf  {R2 activation separates...} }}{32}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bodyseqsupp}{{S5}{32}{}{figure.5}{}}
